### What is the problem before sealed types?

Before sealed types, any type could be extended by anyone, anywhere. The compiler could never know all subtypes. A `switch` or chain of `instanceof` checks always needed a `default` or `else` for unknown subtypes. If a new subtype appeared, the `default` silently swallowed it, often causing a runtime bug.

```java
// Before sealing: open hierarchy
public interface TransactionResult {}
public class Approved implements TransactionResult { /* ... */ }
public class Declined implements TransactionResult { /* ... */ }

// Anyone can add:
public class Pending implements TransactionResult { /* ... */ }

// Switch must have default because set is unknown
String handle(TransactionResult result) {
    return switch (result) {
        case Approved a -> "OK";
        case Declined d -> "Denied";
        default -> "Unknown"; // Silently swallows Pending!
    };
}
```

### How does sealing fix this?

Sealing gives the compiler the complete list of permitted subtypes. A `switch` with a case for each subtype is provably complete, so no `default` is needed.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public sealed interface TransactionResult
    permits Approved, Declined, Pending {}

public record Approved(BigDecimal amount) implements TransactionResult {}
public record Declined(String reason) implements TransactionResult {}
public record Pending(String referenceId) implements TransactionResult {}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.*;

public class ResultHandler {
    public static String handle(TransactionResult result) {
        return switch (result) {
            case Approved a  -> "Approved: " + a.amount();
            case Declined d  -> "Declined: " + d.reason();
            case Pending p   -> "Pending: " + p.referenceId();
            // No default needed: exhaustive!
        };
    }
}
```

### What happens when a new subtype is added?

If a developer adds a new permitted subtype, every exhaustive `switch` that does not handle it fails to compile. This turns a runtime gap into a compile-time error.

```java
// A teammate adds:
public record Reversed(String txnId, String reason)
    implements TransactionResult {}

// Now the permits clause must include Reversed:
public sealed interface TransactionResult
    permits Approved, Declined, Pending, Reversed {}

// COMPILE ERROR in ResultHandler:
// "switch expression does not cover all possible input types"
// Compiler forces you to add: case Reversed r -> ...
```

### Why should you NOT add a default to a sealed switch?

Adding `default` to a sealed switch defeats the safety. The `default` absorbs any new subtype silently, so the compiler no longer warns you.

```java
// BAD: default hides the safety net
return switch (result) {
    case Approved a -> "Approved";
    case Declined d -> "Declined";
    default -> "Other"; // Pending and Reversed silently go here!
};

// GOOD: no default, compiler enforces completeness
return switch (result) {
    case Approved a  -> "Approved: " + a.amount();
    case Declined d  -> "Declined: " + d.reason();
    case Pending p   -> "Pending: " + p.referenceId();
};
```

### How does pattern matching with records work?

Record patterns destructure the components directly in each case, so you bind the fields without any cast.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class PatternExample {
    public static String describe(TransactionResult result) {
        return switch (result) {
            case Approved(var amount)
                -> "Rajesh's transfer of " + amount + " approved.";
            case Declined(var reason)
                -> "Transfer declined: " + reason;
            case Pending(var refId)
                -> "Transfer pending, ref: " + refId;
        };
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(describe(
            new Approved(new BigDecimal("25000.00"))));
        System.out.println(describe(
            new Declined("Insufficient funds")));
    }
}
```

```text
Rajesh's transfer of 25000.00 approved.
Transfer declined: Insufficient funds
```

### How do guarded patterns work with sealed types?

You can refine within the closed set using `when` guards, for example routing high-value approvals to a compliance review.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class ComplianceRouter {
    private static final BigDecimal THRESHOLD = new BigDecimal("100000.00");

    public static String route(TransactionResult result) {
        return switch (result) {
            case Approved(var amt) when amt.compareTo(THRESHOLD) > 0
                -> "High-value approval: route to compliance.";
            case Approved(var amt)
                -> "Standard approval: " + amt;
            case Declined d
                -> "Declined: " + d.reason();
            case Pending p
                -> "Pending: " + p.referenceId();
        };
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(route(
            new Approved(new BigDecimal("500000.00"))));
        System.out.println(route(
            new Approved(new BigDecimal("5000.00"))));
    }
}
```

```text
High-value approval: route to compliance.
Standard approval: 5000.00
```

### How do multi-level sealed hierarchies work?

If a permitted subtype is itself sealed, the compiler expands the set transitively. You can switch on the intermediate sealed type or on its leaves.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public sealed interface Account permits SavingsAccount, CurrentAccount {}
public sealed interface SavingsAccount extends Account
    permits RegularSavings, SeniorSavings {}
public record RegularSavings(BigDecimal balance) implements SavingsAccount {}
public record SeniorSavings(BigDecimal balance) implements SavingsAccount {}
public record CurrentAccount(BigDecimal balance) implements Account {}

// Switch on Account covers all leaves:
// RegularSavings, SeniorSavings, CurrentAccount
```

### What about instanceof with sealed types?

For `instanceof` with pattern matching, sealing bounds the possibilities. While an if-else chain still needs a final `else`, the closed set documents exactly what types are possible.

```java
if (result instanceof Approved a) {
    System.out.println("Amount: " + a.amount());
} else if (result instanceof Declined d) {
    System.out.println("Reason: " + d.reason());
} else if (result instanceof Pending p) {
    System.out.println("Ref: " + p.referenceId());
}
// Modern switch patterns are preferred for exhaustiveness
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The pairing of sealed types with pattern-matching switches is Java's realization of algebraic-data-type pattern matching from functional languages. In banking, where forgetting to handle a new transaction state could cause financial errors, the compile-time guarantee is enormously valuable. Deliberately omit `default` to keep the safety net.
